import { FilesystemReaderCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> FilesystemReaderCopy = FilesystemReaderCopy {
  summary: "The type of the service that performs file operations at runtime. Declare the need with with FileSystem.",
  taskTitle: "Declare the file service a report needs",
  purpose: "A function that writes and reads a report asks its execution environment to perform file operations. It is doing more than calculating text. with FileSystem makes that need visible. A Path describes a destination; FileSystem supplies the operations.",
  setup: "effects, fs, and paths are imported module names. effect fn report directory: paths.Path -> String defines work taking a Path called directory. -> String states the type of the content produced on success. with FileSystem declares the service requirement, and fails ReportError declares ordinary failures. You do not construct the service as an explicit parameter to this function.",
  after: [
    "paths.child builds a Path for report.txt under directory, giving file on success. Its Left contains a path problem and its Right a Path. |> passes the result to the next function; effects.fromEither turns success or failure into executable work. <- executes it and receives file.",
    "let text names an immutable content value, and \\n is a newline. writeTextUtf8 receives CreateNew, the content, and file, in that order. _ <- waits for the write to finish. The final readTextUtf8 produces report’s result. Both operations require the same FileSystem service.",
    "ReportError is this example’s type separating path, write, and read problems as PathFailure, WriteFailure, and ReadFailure. effects.mapError labels each failure accordingly. deriving Show enables display with show.",
    "Executing withTemporaryDirectory from main asks the service to create a directory, pass it to report, and clean it up afterward. effects.attempt and match receive either Right text for success or Left problem for ordinary failure and choose the string that println displays."
  ],
  details: [
    "with FileSystem is shorthand for with fileSystem: FileSystem. The name fileSystem inside Effect<{ fileSystem: FileSystem }, ...> identifies the required service. Its internal representation is not public: a Path or a configuration string cannot stand in for the service.",
    "An import only makes public names available; it does not create a service. This example uses CLI process execution that supplies FileSystem. Successful browser-target compilation and an actual browser execution environment with a file service are different things.",
    "An Effect is a value describing work. Calling report alone does not start writing; execution in an environment with the required service does. Even with that service present, ordinary failures can arise from missing files, permissions, and similar conditions."
  ],
  comparisonBefore: "TS can import node:fs/promises directly, or pass a file-operations object into a function when useful for testing.",
  comparisonAfter: "Seseragi states the service requirement on the function as with FileSystem. The type makes the need visible, while the execution environment must still provide the service."
}
